## Test Smell Audit — `CatalogTest.java`

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 4           | 4              |
| Medium   | 4           | 4              |
| Low      | 2           | 2              |
| **Total**| **10**      | **10**         |

_(1 test is clean — `addThrowsOnNegativeCount`)_

---

### Findings by Severity

#### 🔴 HIGH

**1. Conditional Test Logic** — `addConditionalLogic()` (line 26)

The `if (sku.equals("b"))` branch inside the loop means different SKUs take different paths. A reader can't tell at a glance what quantities were added, and a future change to the array could silently change coverage.

```java
// Smell: branching inside the loop
if (sku.equals("b")) {
    catalog.add(sku, 10);
} else {
    catalog.add(sku, 1);
}
```

**Fix:** Remove the branching; make setup explicit:
```java
@Test
void addConditionalLogic() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    catalog.add("b", 10);
    catalog.add("c", 1);
    assertEquals(1,  catalog.quantity("a"));
    assertEquals(10, catalog.quantity("b"));
    assertEquals(1,  catalog.quantity("c"));
}
```

---

**2. Mystery Guest** — `addLoadsFromFile()` (line 39)

The test creates, writes, and reads a temp file. It depends on `java.nio.file.Files` I/O, making it environment-sensitive and slower than necessary. The data (`"x:7"`) could simply be inlined.

```java
Path tmp = Files.createTempFile("catalog", ".txt");
Files.writeString(tmp, "x:7");
String[] parts = Files.readString(tmp).split(":");
```

**Fix:** Inline the data — no file needed:
```java
@Test
void addLoadsFromFile() {
    catalog.add("x", 7);
    assertTrue(catalog.inStock("x"));
}
```

---

**3. Sleepy Test** — `addSleepy()` (line 49)

`Thread.sleep(200)` adds 200 ms of dead time. `Catalog` is a synchronous in-memory class — the sleep serves no purpose and will accumulate across the suite.

```java
Thread.sleep(200);
```

**Fix:** Delete the sleep:
```java
@Test
void addSleepy() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("a"));
}
```

---

**4. Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)** — `addAndDoNothing()` (line 57)

The test calls `catalog.add("a", 1)` but never asserts anything. It will pass even if `add` silently discards the item, giving false confidence.

```java
@Test
void addAndDoNothing() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
}
```

**Fix:** Assert the expected side-effect:
```java
@Test
void addAndDoNothing() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("a"));
}
```

---

#### 🟡 MEDIUM

**5. Eager Test** — `doEverythingAtOnce()` (line 63)

Calls 4 distinct production methods (`add`, `quantity`, `inStock`, `toString`) in a single test. If it fails, you can't tell which method is broken.

```java
catalog.add("a", 1);
catalog.quantity("a");
catalog.inStock("c");
catalog.toString();
```

**Fix:** Split into focused tests — one per behavior:
```java
@Test
void addAccumulatesQuantity() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    catalog.add("b", 2);
    catalog.add("c", 3);
    assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("a"));
    assertEquals(2, catalog.quantity("b"));
    assertEquals(3, catalog.quantity("c"));
}
```

---

**6. Magic Number Test** — `quantityAfterAdds()` (line 76)

`41` and `42` have no obvious meaning. A reader must mentally compute `41 + 1 = 42` to understand the intent.

```java
catalog.add("a", 41);
catalog.add("a", 1);
assertEquals(42, catalog.quantity("a"));
```

**Fix:** Use self-documenting values or named constants:
```java
@Test
void addAccumulatesQuantityForSameSku() {
    int firstBatch = 5;
    int secondBatch = 3;
    catalog.add("a", firstBatch);
    catalog.add("a", secondBatch);
    assertEquals(firstBatch + secondBatch, catalog.quantity("a"));
}
```

